import Foundation

let toolsSection = RefSection(
    id: "tools", title: "Curves, Surfaces & Patterns", icon: "scribble.variable",
    blurb: "What each of the Extras tools draws or simulates, with the mathematics behind it.",
    groups: [
        RefGroup("Curves", [
            rx("tool.lissajous", "Lissajous curve", "The path traced by a point whose x and y coordinates oscillate sinusoidally at different frequencies. If a : b is a ratio of integers the curve closes, with a lobes across the top and b down the side; the phase φ sets how ‘open’ the figure is. Seen on oscilloscopes, and in the paths of a double-pendulum drawing machine (harmonograph).", "x = sin(a·t),  y = sin(b·t + φ)"),
            rx("tool.rose", "Rose curve (rhodonea)", "A polar curve r = cos(kθ). For integer k it has k petals if k is odd and 2k if even. For k = p/q in lowest terms it closes after q turns (θ from 0 to 2πq); for irrational k it never closes. k = 1 is a circle.", "r = cos(kθ)"),
            rx("tool.spirograph", "Spirograph (hypotrochoid & epitrochoid)", "The curve drawn by a pen attached to a gear of radius r that rolls inside (hypotrochoid) or outside (epitrochoid) a fixed ring of radius R, with the pen d from the gear’s centre. The curve closes after r/gcd(R, r) turns and has R/gcd(R, r) lobes. d = r gives the cusped hypocycloid or epicycloid.", "hypo: x = (R − r)cos t + d·cos((R − r)t/r)\n       y = (R − r)sin t − d·sin((R − r)t/r)"),
            rx("tool.spacefilling", "Space-filling curves", "Continuous curves that pass through every point of a square. The Hilbert curve (1891) subdivides into four quadrants at each order; the Peano curve (1890) into nine. At order n the Hilbert curve visits a 2ⁿ × 2ⁿ grid and the Peano curve a 3ⁿ × 3ⁿ grid. The limit curve is continuous but nowhere differentiable and has dimension 2."),
            rx("tool.epicycles", "Fourier epicycles", "Any closed path, treated as a complex-valued function of time, is a sum of rotating circles. The discrete Fourier transform of the sampled path gives each circle’s radius (its coefficient’s magnitude) and starting angle; circle k turns k times per cycle. Chaining the circles from largest to smallest, the tip retraces the path. This is the idea of Ptolemy’s epicycles made exact. Corners need many circles, which is the Gibbs phenomenon again.", "z(t) = Σₖ cₖ e^{2πikt},  cₖ = (1/N) Σₙ zₙ e^{−2πikn/N}"),
        ]),
        RefGroup("Chaos & dynamics", [
            rx("tool.logistic", "Logistic map", "The simplest chaotic system: xₙ₊₁ = r·xₙ(1 − xₙ), a model of a population with limited food. For r < 1 it dies out, for 1 < r < 3 it settles to one value, then period-doubles at r = 3, 3.449…, 3.544…, reaching chaos at r∞ ≈ 3.5699. At r = 4 the whole interval is explored, and windows of order (such as the period-3 window near r = 3.83) are scattered through the chaos.", "xₙ₊₁ = r·xₙ(1 − xₙ)"),
            rx("tool.cobweb", "Cobweb diagram", "A graphical way to follow xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ): from the point (x₀, 0) go vertically to the curve, then horizontally to the diagonal y = x, and repeat. A fixed point is where the curve meets the diagonal; the web spirals into it if |f′| < 1 there and away if |f′| > 1."),
            rx("tool.bifurcation", "Bifurcation diagram", "Plots the long-run values of the logistic map against r. At each r the map is run for a while to settle, then the next iterates are recorded. A single line splits into two (period 2), then four, eight and so on until chaos fills a band, which is itself interrupted by narrow windows of order. Every window contains a miniature copy of the whole diagram."),
            rx("tool.feigenbaum", "Feigenbaum constants", "The lengths of successive period-doubling intervals shrink by a factor approaching δ ≈ 4.669201609…, and the widths of the branches shrink by α ≈ 2.502907875…. Both are universal: the same numbers appear for every smooth one-hump map, and have been measured in fluid and electronic experiments. From the first doubling points the ratios are 4.75, 4.66, 4.67, …"),
            rx("tool.attractor", "Strange attractor", "The set a chaotic dissipative system’s trajectories settle onto. It has zero volume, fractal structure, and sensitive dependence: nearby trajectories on it separate exponentially but never leave it. The twin trajectory in the tool shows this directly."),
            rx("tool.lorenz", "Lorenz system", "Edward Lorenz’s 1963 simplified model of atmospheric convection. For σ = 10, β = 8/3 and ρ above about 24.74 the motion is chaotic and traces a two-lobed butterfly attractor with fractal dimension about 2.06. The ‘butterfly effect’ takes its name from its sensitivity to initial conditions.", "ẋ = σ(y − x),  ẏ = x(ρ − z) − y,  ż = xy − βz"),
            rx("tool.rossler", "Rössler system", "Otto Rössler’s 1976 attractor, designed as the simplest continuous chaotic flow: one spiral lobe and an occasional fold up out of the plane. With a = b = 0.2 and c = 5.7 it is chaotic; lowering c gives period-doubling to a simple loop.", "ẋ = −y − z,  ẏ = x + a·y,  ż = b + z(x − c)"),
            rx("tool.threebody", "Three-body problem", "Three masses attracting each other by Newtonian gravity. Unlike the two-body problem it has no general closed-form solution (Poincaré, 1890s) and is chaotic for most starts. Special solutions exist: the Lagrange equilateral triangle (unstable for equal masses), Euler’s collinear solutions and the figure-eight (Chenciner & Montgomery, 2000), in which three equal masses chase each other along one curve. The tool integrates with RK4 and an adaptive step that shrinks in close encounters; the energy drift shown is the integration error.", "r̈ᵢ = Σⱼ G mⱼ (rⱼ − rᵢ) / |rⱼ − rᵢ|³"),
            rx("tool.pythagorean", "Pythagorean three-body problem", "Masses 3, 4 and 5 placed at the corners of a 3-4-5 right triangle, opposite their sides, and released from rest. After a complicated dance with many close encounters, one body is ejected (Burrau, 1913; solved numerically by Szebehely & Peters, 1967)."),
        ]),
        RefGroup("Hyperbolic geometry", [
            rx("tool.hyperbolic", "Hyperbolic tilings {p, q}", "Tilings of the hyperbolic plane by regular p-sided polygons with q meeting at every vertex. In the Euclidean plane only {3,6}, {4,4} and {6,3} work; the hyperbolic plane allows infinitely many, exactly when (p − 2)(q − 2) > 4. Each tile is congruent (same hyperbolic size), so tiles near the rim look smaller only because of how the disk model compresses distance. The tool builds the tiling by reflecting the central polygon across its edges.", "(p − 2)(q − 2) > 4"),
            rx("tool.poincare", "Poincaré disk model", "A map of the whole hyperbolic plane onto the inside of a unit disk. Lines are circular arcs meeting the boundary at right angles (or diameters), distances grow without limit toward the rim, and angles are shown true. The isometries are the Möbius transformations of the disk, which is what dragging applies.", "d(z, w) = arccosh(1 + 2|z − w|² / ((1 − |z|²)(1 − |w|²)))"),
            rx("tool.escher", "Escher and hyperbolic tilings", "M. C. Escher’s Circle Limit woodcuts (1958–60) fill the Poincaré disk with congruent angels, devils, fish and more. They were inspired by H. S. M. Coxeter’s diagrams of hyperbolic triangle tilings. The ‘slices’ option splits each tile into triangles in alternating shades, in the spirit of those diagrams."),
        ]),
        RefGroup("Cellular automata & reaction–diffusion", [
            rx("tool.life", "Conway’s Game of Life", "A cellular automaton on a grid devised by John Conway (1970). Each cell is alive or dead and updates by looking at its eight neighbours: a live cell survives with 2 or 3 live neighbours; a dead cell becomes alive with exactly 3. Despite the simple rules it supports gliders, oscillators, guns that emit gliders (the Gosper gun), and universal computation. The R-pentomino, five cells, takes 1103 generations to settle.", "survive: 2 or 3 neighbours;  born: exactly 3"),
            rx("tool.elementary", "Elementary cellular automata", "A one-dimensional row of cells, each updated from itself and its two neighbours: 2³ = 8 possible neighbourhoods, each mapped to 0 or 1, so there are 2⁸ = 256 rules, numbered by reading the eight outputs as a binary number (Wolfram’s numbering). Each new row is drawn below the last, so time runs downward."),
            rx("tool.rule30", "Rule 30", "An elementary automaton (00011110₂) that turns a single black cell into a chaotic triangle. Its centre column looks random and was used as a random number generator (in Mathematica). The left side is regular, the right side irregular."),
            rx("tool.rule110", "Rule 110", "An elementary automaton (01101110₂) with localised structures that move and collide against a regular background. Matthew Cook proved in 2004 that it is Turing-complete: in principle it can compute anything computable. Rule 90 gives the Sierpiński triangle."),
            rx("tool.langton", "Langton’s ant", "A two-dimensional Turing machine (Chris Langton, 1986): an ant on a grid turns right on a white cell and left on a coloured one, flips the cell’s colour and steps forward. After about 10,000 steps of seemingly chaotic wandering it builds a repeating diagonal ‘highway’ with period 104. Whether every starting pattern ends in a highway is unproven."),
            rx("tool.grayscott", "Gray–Scott reaction–diffusion", "Two chemicals U and V diffuse and react: U + 2V → 3V converts U to V, U is fed in at rate F, and V is removed at rate F + k. The equations produce spots, stripes, labyrinths, spirals and splitting cells, depending on F and k. The simulation runs explicit finite differences on a torus with a nine-point Laplacian.", "∂u/∂t = Dᵤ∇²u − uv² + F(1 − u)\n∂v/∂t = Dᵥ∇²v + uv² − (F + k)v"),
            rx("tool.turing", "Turing patterns", "Alan Turing’s 1952 insight: two diffusing, reacting substances can spontaneously form stable spatial patterns from an almost uniform start, if one diffuses faster than the other. This ‘diffusion-driven instability’ is a proposed mechanism for animal coat patterns, fingerprints and shell patterns. Gray–Scott is a different system with the same flavour."),
        ]),
        RefGroup("Surfaces", [
            rx("tool.mobius", "Möbius strip", "A band with one half-twist, glued end to end: it has one side and one edge and is non-orientable. Cut down the middle it stays in one piece (with a full twist). With an even number of half-twists the band has two sides. Discovered independently by Möbius and Listing in 1858.", "x = (1 + (w/2)v cos(nu/2)) cos u,  y = (…) sin u,  z = (w/2)v sin(nu/2)"),
            rx("tool.klein", "Klein bottle", "A closed non-orientable surface with no inside or outside and no boundary, obtained by gluing two Möbius strips along their edges. It cannot be embedded in 3D without passing through itself; the figure-eight form shown is an immersion. In 4D it embeds without self-intersection. Its Euler characteristic is 0."),
            rx("tool.minimal", "Minimal surface", "A surface whose mean curvature is zero everywhere, so it locally minimises area. Soap films stretched on a wire frame are minimal surfaces (Plateau’s problem). Examples: the plane, the catenoid, the helicoid, Enneper’s surface and Scherk’s surfaces. They are saddle-shaped everywhere: the two principal curvatures are equal and opposite."),
            rx("tool.catenoid", "Catenoid", "The surface of revolution of a catenary (a hanging chain’s curve): the soap film between two parallel rings. It is the only minimal surface of revolution apart from the plane (Euler, 1744).", "x = a cosh(v/a) cos u,  y = a cosh(v/a) sin u,  z = v"),
            rx("tool.helicoid", "Helicoid", "The ruled surface swept by a line that rotates at constant speed as it rises, like a spiral staircase or a screw. It is a minimal surface and, with the plane, the only ruled one (Catalan, 1842).", "x = v cos u,  y = v sin u,  z = c·u"),
            rx("tool.associate", "Catenoid–helicoid deformation", "The catenoid and helicoid are locally isometric: one can be bent into the other without stretching, through a family of minimal surfaces. The tool animates this family parametrised by θ.", "θ = π/2: catenoid,  θ = 0: helicoid"),
            rx("tool.enneper", "Enneper’s surface", "A self-intersecting minimal surface found by Alfred Enneper in 1864, given by a simple polynomial parametrisation. The patch of small extent is a graceful saddle; larger extents curl and cross themselves.", "x = u − u³/3 + uv²,  y = v − v³/3 + vu²,  z = u² − v²"),
            rx("tool.scherk", "Scherk’s surface", "Scherk’s first surface (1834) is a doubly periodic minimal surface, the graph of ln(cos y / cos x) over a square; copies glue into a checkerboard of saddles that looks like two sets of intersecting sheets.", "z = ln(cos y / cos x)"),
        ]),
        RefGroup("Number patterns", [
            rx("tool.modular", "Modular multiplication circle", "Place n points evenly around a circle, label them 0 to n − 1, and join each point k to the point m·k mod n. For m = 2 the chords envelope a cardioid; m = 3 gives a nephroid; in general m − 1 cusps appear. Non-integer m gives continuous families of curves and shows how the pattern changes between integers. The picture is a visual form of the multiplication table modulo n."),
            rx("tool.cardioid", "Cardioid & nephroid", "A cardioid is the heart-shaped envelope with one cusp, also the outline of the Mandelbrot set’s main body. A nephroid has two cusps. Both arise as envelopes of chords of a circle and as epicycloids.", "cardioid = epicycloid with R = r;  nephroid = epicycloid with R = 2r"),
            rx("tool.ulam", "Ulam spiral", "Write the positive integers in a square spiral and mark the primes. Stanisław Ulam noticed in 1963, while doodling during a talk, that the primes fall along diagonal lines. The diagonals correspond to quadratic polynomials that produce many primes, such as n² + n + 41 (Euler’s prime-generating polynomial). Why they arise is understood only heuristically."),
            rx("tool.sacks", "Sacks spiral", "Robert Sacks’s 1994 variant: place n at radius √n and angle 2π√n, so perfect squares lie along a horizontal ray and the integers wind in an Archimedean spiral. Primes then line up along curved arms that correspond to quadratic polynomials, and some arms are conspicuously empty."),
            rx("tool.taylor", "Taylor and Maclaurin series", "A smooth function can be approximated near a point a by the polynomial with the same derivatives there: f(x) ≈ Σ f⁽ᵏ⁾(a)(x − a)ᵏ/k!. At a = 0 it is called a Maclaurin series. The series for sin x, cos x and eˣ converge for every x; the series for ln(1 + x), arctan x and 1/(1 − x) have radius of convergence 1, so adding more terms makes the approximation worse outside (−1, 1).", "sin x = x − x³/3! + x⁵/5! − …"),
            rx("tool.fourier", "Fourier series", "A periodic function is a sum of sines and cosines whose frequencies are whole multiples of a fundamental frequency. The square wave’s series contains only odd harmonics with amplitudes 1/k, the sawtooth’s contains all harmonics, and the triangle wave’s amplitudes fall as 1/k² (faster, since it is continuous). More terms give a closer fit in the middle but a persistent overshoot at jumps.", "square(x) = (4/π) Σ sin((2k − 1)x)/(2k − 1)"),
            rx("tool.gibbs", "Gibbs phenomenon", "Near a jump discontinuity, partial sums of a Fourier series overshoot by about 8.95% of the jump (0.179 for a jump of 2), and this overshoot does not shrink as more terms are added; it only narrows and moves closer to the jump. Smoothing the coefficients (Fejér averaging, or Lanczos σ-factors as in the tool) removes most of it at the price of a softer edge.", "overshoot → (1/π) Si(π) − ½ ≈ 0.0895 of the jump"),
        ]),
    ])
